<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Council of Florence</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1438">1438</a>–<a href="/years/1445">1445</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Council of Ferrara-Florence</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> was an ecumenical council of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> that met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> from 1438 and in Florence from 1439, continuing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-basel">Council of Basel</a>. Attended by the Byzantine emperor John VIII Palaiologos and the Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, it proclaimed the union of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek churches in July 1439, but the union was rejected by most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> clergy and laity and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/council-of-florence.jpg" alt="Council of Florence" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michel Wolgemut, Wilhelm Pleydenwurff (Hartmann Schedel, editor) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nuremberg_chronicles_f_244v_2_Concilium_florentinum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The council grew out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-basel">Council of Basel</a>, convoked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-martin-v">Pope Martin V</a> shortly before his death in February 1431 and seated on 14 December 1431, when the conciliar movement was strong and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> weak. It took place amid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussite-wars">Hussite Wars</a> and the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, and at stake was the conflict between the conciliar movement and papal supremacy. The fathers at Basel proclaimed the superiority of a council over the pope. In December 1431 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-eugene-iv">Pope Eugene IV</a> tried to dissolve it and open a new council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> but was overruled, and in December 1433 he conceded the point in a bull.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>After the death of Emperor Sigismund in 1437, Eugene IV translated the council to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> on 8 January 1438, where it drew some of the Byzantine delegates who had been at Basel. Some members rejected the papal decree and stayed at Basel, where this rump council suspended Eugene and in November 1439 elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadeus-viii-duke-of-savoy">Amadeus VIII, Duke of Savoy</a>, as the antipope Felix V. The council moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1439, because of plague at Ferrara and because Florence agreed to finance it.</p><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> the delegates reached agreements on the Western insertion of the Filioque into the creed, the number of the sacraments and Purgatory. Papal supremacy and the promise of military aid against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> were also at issue. On 6 July 1439 the union was proclaimed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek in the document Laetentur Caeli, signed by Eugene IV and the emperor John VIII Palaiologos. Some Greek bishops accepted it reluctantly, and others, such as Isidore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, out of conviction. Mark of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> refused and led the opposition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Delegates of the patriarchates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> signed, but their patriarchs later rejected the union. The Serbian Patriarch Teofan I did not attend, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a> rejected the union on learning of it, although Avraamy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> had signed. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> the great majority of clergy and laity rejected it, accusing the emperor and the patriarch of betrayal. Western military help proved insufficient, and Constantinople fell in May 1453.</p><p>The council declared the Basel group heretics, and in the bull Etsi non dubitemus of 20 April 1441 it affirmed the superiority of the pope over councils. The rump council was driven out of Basel in 1448, moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a> and closed in 1449, when Felix V resigned.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Council of Florence” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Basilios Bessarion, born in Trebizond around 1403, became a leading theologian, a supporter of the union of the churches at the <b>Council of Florence</b> and a cardinal of the Roman Church, whose library founded the Biblioteca Marciana in Venice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In church affairs, Moscow rejected the union with Rome agreed at the <b>Council of Florence</b> in 1439, and in 1448 Russian bishops chose Jonah as metropolitan without approval from Constantinople, a de facto independence confirmed after the Ottoman conquest of Constantinople in 1453.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Council of Florence</b> of 1439 brought Byzantine scholars to the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
